# python3 bin/python/euler_castles.py  (2026-10-28, Python 3.11)
[1] every Euler castle with w <= 8, h <= 5 has distinct row lengths, rows = its h blocks, bottom row = w; row lengths <-> distinct-part partitions, one-to-one onto Euler castles of area n <= 40: True
[1] example (3, 3, 2, 1, 1): rows (5, 3, 2), blocks 3, area 10
[2] Euler castles of area n = prod (1 + q^k) = A000009(n), n <= 40: True
[2] by area, n = 1..16: [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 32]
[2] A_Euler(w, h) = C(w - 1, h - 1): brute force over all skylines w <= 8, h <= 5, and from the area lists for w(w + 1)/2 <= 40: True
[2] by width 2^(w-1) (sum of the cell counts); by semi-perimeter s = w + h: F(s - 1), s <= 27 from the cell counts and s <= 9 from the castles: True
[3] even - odd Euler castles of area n = (-1)^k at n = k(3k +- 1)/2, 0 otherwise, = prod (1 - q^k) = A010815(n), n <= 40: True
[3] even-block = A067661(n), odd-block = A067659(n), n <= 26: True
[3] even-block by area, n = 1..16: [0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9, 11, 13, 16]
[3] odd-block by area,  n = 1..16: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 14, 16]
[4] the move maps Euler castles to Euler castles of the same area, is its own inverse, changes the height by 1, keeps w + blocks, and is Franklin's rule on the rows (ascending); undefined exactly once at each pentagonal area, on (k^k, k-1, ..., 1) and (k^(k+1), k-1, ..., 1); n <= 40: True
